# for (i = 0; i < bound; i++) { up(i, 2); remainder(i % 10, 2); kept = i; onlyAdded(i, 2); small(i); behindTest(i, 2) }
# for (i = 5; i > bound; i--) down(i, 2)   for (i = 0; i < bound; i += 2) byTwo(i, 2)   for (i = 0.5; i < bound; i++) fromFraction(i, 2)
# function up(i, depth) { if (depth > 0) return up(i, depth - 1); return i % 8 }   and so the others, but for
# function onlyAdded(i, depth) { .. return i + 8 }   function small(i) { return i % 8 }   function behindTest(i, depth) { .. if (i < 3) return 0; return i % 8 }
# `bound` is a number of which nothing is known. At 2 ** 53 a step of one is lost to rounding, so a counter that starts at an integer and goes by steps of one is an integer from -2 ** 53 to 2 ** 53 however long the loop runs.
# The loop keeps its counter as it was, a number without a fraction: a double takes a step at no cost. What it hands to a function that stays a function, and that does with it what an integer is better for,
# arrives as an integer, and so does what is reckoned from it.
# A function that calls itself is inlined nowhere. It hands its parameter what the parameter holds: nothing that no other call has handed it.
# A step of two leaves the range, and a fraction was never in it. These stay as they were: a variable that is stored the counter; the parameter of a function that only adds to it, which a double takes at no cost;
# the parameter of a function that is small enough to be inlined, whose body reckons with the counter as the loop keeps it.
